# Technical Analysis

A secure password reset workflow should behave as follows:

```text
Password Reset Request
        │
        ▼
Generate Random Token
        │
        ▼
Store Token Server-Side
        │
        ▼
User Submits Token
        │
        ▼
Validate Token
        │
   Valid ─────► Update Password
 Invalid ────► Reject Request
```

Instead, the application behaves similarly to:

```text
Receive Request
        │
        ▼
Read username
        │
        ▼
Update Password
```

The reset token is present in both the URL and request body but is never validated before updating the user's password.

---

# Root Cause

The server relies solely on the `username` parameter when processing password reset requests.

Although password reset tokens are generated and delivered to users, they are ignored during password update operations.

---

# Impact

An attacker can:

- Reset arbitrary user passwords.
- Take over any account.
- Bypass email ownership verification.
- Gain unauthorized access to sensitive information.
- Completely compromise the application.

